{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== REMEDIATION VALIDATED + PROCESS BUG FILED (final) ===\n\nEpic 1386 re-review after remediation: PASS, 0 blocking, 11 advisory; fresh attestation signed at sha 2281280d. It immediately reads stale-code again because det_floor.py drifted when 35bc landed - documented moving-base-ref behaviour, not a defect. The epic needs ONE final review-plan at close time once all children have landed; chasing it earlier is wasted because every child landing re-stales it.\n\nFILED BUG e7be-b985-c344-46f7 (creamy-cocksure-elkhound): 'Plan review certified an epic whose acceptance criteria were mutually unsatisfiable'. Linked discovered_from 1386, relates_to 35bc and 2aa6. Gates: check-ac pass (6 criteria), clarity 7/5 pass, quality pass.\n\nThe bug records SEVEN unsatisfiable criteria across epic 1386, 35bc and 2aa6 - all of which PASSED plan review with 0 blocking (epic manifest: plan-review PASS / blocking 0 / advisory 11) - in four classes:\n- A: an AC contradicting its own ticket's Scope or a sibling AC IN THE SAME DOCUMENT. Sharpest example: 2aa6 AC1 demanded zero SC matches across src/rebar/llm/reviewers/ while its own AC6 demanded exactly one match in plan_review_T2.md inside that directory.\n- B: an AC contradicting a mechanical property of a generated artifact. Epic AC5 required the criteria guide be regenerated from the registry AND contain no SC vocabulary, when the registry contains the excluded T2 prompt.\n- C: an AC hard-coding a count (epic AC4 'the 108 store tickets') that the plan elsewhere says to recompute; actual was 106.\n- D: a parent AC covered by NO child (llm/completion.py:288). Recorded as an observation: criterion G3 exists precisely to check 'the union of children covers each parent acceptance/success criterion', routes at container scope, ran, and did not flag it.\n\nAlso recorded: the reviewer DID block 2aa6 on re-review for a stale cross-ticket factual claim (asserting 1f32 had not merged when it had), so cross-ticket fact-checking fires; the seven failures are the different, internal-consistency class. And the cost framing: plan review certifies plans the completion verifier later refuses, so the disagreement surfaces after the code is written, reviewed and in two cases already merged, with every correction forcing a full LLM re-review via stale-material.\n\nPer operator instruction the bug records evidence ONLY and does not diagnose the plan-review process; a follow-up session owns that."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785019911976531001,"uuid":"34809a06-710f-4c79-b134-66220e72fbc1"}